A||||6||GT Bastard||I need a transmission expert....  TH125 shift issues||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-12-2001||08:12 PM||||Here's the deal:  Fierogod put a 3.1 V6 and a Corsica TH125 into an '85 Fiero.  Supposedly, the Corsica tranny is a "lightweight" version of the TH125 with a couple different features that I'm not sure of.  The problem is that the tranny won't kickdown.  It doesn't matter if you're on the throttle or not.  Everyone is telling him that it's the cable, but he says it moves freely... Any ideas?  The tranny has new fluid and filter.  <P>Thanks in advance fellas...||||reg||4
Z||000001||87GTZ34||02-12-2001||09:49 PM||||GTB - The TV cable adjustment is very touchy on that transaxle.  I had mine (cable only) apart for cleaning, put it back together and shifting was erratic.  It took a few tries following the GM manual adjustment method but it finally worked.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||02-13-2001||02:14 PM||||Is there any special trick to adjusting it properly?<P>thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||02-13-2001||03:13 PM||||remember, adjustment of the TV cable must be made by rotating the throtle lever at the throttle body. Do not use the accelerator pedal to rotate the throttle lever. The engine must be off during this adjustment.<P>1. the free movement of the TV cable can be checked by pulling the upper end of the cable. The cable should travel a short distance with light resistance due to the small coiled return spring. Pull the cable farther out to move the lever into contact with the plunger, thus compressing the heavier TV spring. When released, the cable should return to the closed position.<P>2. Depress and hold down the metal re-adjust tab at the engine end of the TV cable<P>3. While holding the tab down, move the slider until it stops against the fitting.<P>4. Release and readjust tab.<P>5. Rotate the throttle lever to the maximum travel stop position. The cable will ratchet through the slider and automatically readjust itself.<P>6. Make sure that the cable moves freely. It may appear to function properly with the engine cold. Recheck it after the engine is hot.<P>7. Road test the vehicle. If delayed or only full-throttle shifts still occur, it may be something more than a mis-adjusted TV cable - the problem could be internall.<P>if you want we could take a look at it if your nearby<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-13-2001||08:05 PM||||A "light" version of the 125.... That's a new one.<P>There are several things that can screw up a 125. Start with getting the TV cable set right.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROGOD||02-15-2001||12:39 AM|||| Ok i talked to the pontiac trans tech at our local dealer and a tech at a trans shop. They both said to check the tv cable. This seems to work fine. I think the problem is with the pressure switchs. The new style 125 (the trans people refered to it as a light-weight 125) has second gear lock up. It has i think two pressure switchs. One for the convertor and one for kick down. But i am not sure. Has anyone put the new style 125 in a fiero? It also has a four wire four pin connector for the lock up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-15-2001||07:46 PM||||The cable may be moving but it still may not be working right inside the tranny. The diagrams I have on the older version show the linkage doesn't workt the valve directly. there is a little bell crank type arangement that operates the actual valve.<P>A second gear lockup shouldn't keep it from kicking down. At least I don't think it would.<P>I think at this point you need to find the book for that version of tranny or take into a shop. I seem to remember these guys have books for most tranny. I haven't checked recently.<A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>Transmission Exchange Co.</A> You could also try someone who has alldata and seeing if the tranny has any TSBs on it that apply to your problem.||||reg||1
